{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:MassEndomorphism.MarkovTraceAntipodePolynomialSymmetry","@type":"LeanModule","name":"MassEndomorphism.MarkovTraceAntipodePolynomialSymmetry","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Reverse","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.markovTraceAntipodePolynomialSymmetry","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.markovTraceAntipodePolynomialSymmetry","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-endomorphism/markov-trace-antipode-polynomial-symmetry.lean#L64-L96","doc":"**Anti-self-reciprocity of the proton closed-form polynomial.**\n\nThe polynomial `p(q) = q^20 + q^18 + 2q^12 - 2q^8 - q^2 - 1`\nsatisfies the polynomial identity\n\n  shiftedReverse20 p = -p,\n\nwhich is the polynomial avatar of the antipode-induced\nrelation `q^20 · p(1/q) = -p(q)` on the closed-form Markov\ntrace.\n\n**Proof.**  Both sides are concrete elements of `ℤ[X]`.  Expanding\nthe degree-20 shifted reverse over `Finset.range 21` and computing\neach `protonClosedFormPolynomial.coeff (20 - k)` (only the six\nnon-zero coefficients survive) reduces the goal to a polynomial\nidentity in `X` that `ring` closes.  No axiom and no `sorry`.\n\nCategorical content (delegated to references): the Hopf antipode\n`S(σ_i) = σ_i^{-1}` on `H_3(q)` descends through the Frobenius\nintegral / Markov trace to this polynomial identity on the\nclosed-form numerator.  The minus sign reflects the odd parity\nof the proton word's length 11 under the antipode-induced\ninvolution. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:markov-trace-antipode-polynomial-symmetry","label":"prop:markov-trace-antipode-polynomial-symmetry","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-endomorphism/markov-trace-antipode-polynomial-symmetry.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.shiftedReverse20","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.shiftedReverse20","kind":"def","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-endomorphism/markov-trace-antipode-polynomial-symmetry.lean#L56-L62","doc":"The \"shifted reverse\" of a polynomial of degree d:\n`q^d · p(1/q)` rewritten as a polynomial in q.\nFor our polynomial of degree 20, this is the operation\nthat interchanges coefficient at degree k with coefficient at\ndegree (20 - k). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.protonClosedFormPolynomial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.protonClosedFormPolynomial","kind":"def","line":47,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-endomorphism/markov-trace-antipode-polynomial-symmetry.lean#L47-L54","doc":"The numerator polynomial of the closed-form Markov trace\n`tr_M(β_p) = p(q) / (12 q^10)` from PR #546.\n\nCoefficients: degree 0 = -1, degree 2 = -1, degree 8 = -2,\ndegree 12 = +2, degree 18 = +1, degree 20 = +1.\nAll other coefficients are zero. ","signatureChunk":0}]}